<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harris Papyrus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Harris <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> is a long Egyptian papyrus from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>'s successor, recording the king's donations to temples. It shows that the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> owned vast lands and resources. It is a key source for the economic power of the priesthood in the late Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harris-papyrus.jpg" alt="Harris Papyrus" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papyrus_Harris_I_Pl._LXXVI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Known also as the Great Harris Papyrus and catalogued as Papyrus British Museum EA 9999, the roll is 41 metres long, the longest known papyrus from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, with about 1,500 lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> on 79 pages. Arab finders showed it to the collector Harris in 1855 among a sackful of papyri, which they said came from a cave in the small valley that leads from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-medina">Deir el-Medina</a>. The cache probably came from the archives of the temple of Medinet Habu, the administrative centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>. Birch published a facsimile of the hieratic text in 1876, and Pierre Grandet published a full edition in 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text opens with year 32 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> and lists his pious foundations throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, in three groups: prayers to the gods followed by his buildings and gifts; the wealth of the temples, from their tax income and royal gifts to festival offerings; and a closing prayer with historical recollections. The lists of assets centre on the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, of Ra at Heliopolis and of Memphis, recording livestock, gardens, fields, ships, shipyards and towns given as perpetual possessions.</p><p>In the last five pages the king speaks of his reign. He tells of a civil war at the end of the 19th Dynasty that led to the founding of the 20th, of an invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, and of his defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> and the Meshwesh, whose captives, he says, were branded and made slaves and whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> went to the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. The final page passes his titles and throne to his son Ramesses IV, in whose reign the roll was written.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>By the figures the roll gives, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> administered 80 per cent of the temple land and 86 per cent of the temple livestock. Scholars have read the lists as showing that the temples were very rich, but not necessarily so rich as to threaten the state, so that the later rise of the high priests of Amun needs other causes as well. The full account of the dead king's merits suggests that politics shaped the roll's purpose. At least three scribes, from Thebes, Heliopolis and Memphis, wrote it in haste, in parts glued together.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Papyrus Harris I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the reign of Ramesses III, according to the <b>Great Harris Papyrus</b>, the temples of Amun held roughly two-thirds of all temple lands in Egypt and some ninety percent of temple-owned ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings gave them fields, captives and the spoils of war, above all to Amun of Karnak, and modern reckonings from the <b>Harris Papyrus</b> and the Wilbour Papyrus put roughly 13–18 per cent of Egypt’s farmland in their hands under the Ramesside kings, most of it Amun’s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
